  <content>
  <section><title>Activity </title>
<span id="docs-internal-guid-180eef2b-7fff-2dde-333d-ca8fa2851b02"> Consider the functions \(f(x)=2x\) and
  \(g(x)=(1.01)^x\).</span><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Complete the table of values for the functions \(f\) and \(g\). </para>
    <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <thead>
        <row>
          <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
            \(x\)
          </entry>
          <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
            \(f(x)\)
          </entry>
          <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
            \(g(x)\)
          </entry>
        </row>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <entry>
            1
          </entry>
          <entry/>
          <entry/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            10
          </entry>
          <entry/>
          <entry/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            50
          </entry>
          <entry/>
          <entry/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            100
          </entry>
          <entry/>
          <entry/>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            500
          </entry>
          <entry/>
          <entry/>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <thead>
        <row>
          <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
            \(x\)
          </entry>
          <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
            \(f(x)\)
          </entry>
          <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
            \(g(x)\)
          </entry>
        </row>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <entry>
            1
          </entry>
          <entry>
            2
          </entry>
          <entry>
            1.01
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            10
          </entry>
          <entry>
            20
          </entry>
          <entry>
            ~1.10
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            50
          </entry>
          <entry>
            100
          </entry>
          <entry>
            ~1.645
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            100
          </entry>
          <entry>
            200
          </entry>
          <entry>
            ~2.7
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            500
          </entry>
          <entry>
            1,000
          </entry>
          <entry>
            ~144.8
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Based on the table of values, which function do you think grows faster? Be prepared to show your reasoning.
      </para>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\(f\) is growing faster for the values of \(x\) in the table, but
    as \(x\) increases, \(g\) starts to grow faster. So
    as \(x\) increases, \(g\) may eventually grow faster than \(f\). </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Which function do you think will reach a value of 2,000 first? Show your reasoning. If you get stuck, consider
        increasing \(x\) by 100 a few times and record the function values in the table. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>When \(x\) is 600, \(f(600)=1,200\) and \(g(600) \approx 392\), so \(f\) takes a larger value.
      When \(x\) is 700, \(f(700)=1,400\) and \(g(700) \approx 1,059\), so \(f\) still takes a larger value.
      But when \(x\) is 800, \(f(800)=1,600\) and \(g(800) \approx 2,865\), so \(g\) takes a larger value.
    </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
